2 CONT
2 CONT [S954] Esq., Bengal Civil Service William Muir Muir on Mahomet, Vol. 2, Chap. 2
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruqayyah is viewed as the daughter of Muhammad and Khadijah bint Khuwaylid by Sunni Muslims[citation needed], other Muslim sects such as Shia Muslims debate her being daughter of Muhammed (or even of Khadijah) (see Genealogy of Kh
2 CONC adijas daughters). She died in 2 AH (624 CE).[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Marriage & children 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Migration to Abyssinia 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Dispute over being daughter of Muhammad &/or Khadijah 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 See also 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage & children
2 CONT
2 CONT She was first married to Utbah ibn Abu Lahab. His father, Abu Lahab, forced Utbah to repudiate Ruqayyah due to Abu Lahab's opposition to Muhammad and his teachings. Muslim convert Uthman ibn Affan had long admired Ruqayyah and was
2 CONC  then able to ask for her hand in marriage.[1] Ruqayyah's son was Abd-Allah ibn Uthman, who died when he was 2 years old.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Migration to Abyssinia
2 CONT
2 CONT She participated in the Migration to Abyssinia.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Dispute over being daughter of Muhammad &/or Khadijah
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Genealogy of Khadijah's daughters
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughters attributed to Muhammad are;
2 CONT
2 CONT Zainab bint Muhammad, married to her maternal cousin Abu al-Aas ibn al-Rabee before al-Hijra 
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughters attributed to Muhammad are;
2 CONT
2 CONT Zainab bint Muhammad, married to her maternal cousin Abu al-Aas ibn al-Rabee before al-Hijra 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruqayyah bint Muhammad, was first married to Utbah ibn Abu Lahab & then to Uthman ibn Affan 
2 CONT
2 CONT Umm Kulthum bint Muhammad, was first married to Utaybah bin Abu Lahab & then to Uthman ibn Affan after the death of her sister Ruqayyah 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fatimah, was married to Ali ('Ali bin Abi Talib) 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to some Shia Muslim sources she only had one daughter, Fatimah. The others either belonged to her sister[2] or were orphaned girls raised by her[3]. Possibly, all of them were Khadijah's but only Fatimah was born to Muha
2 CONC mmad.[citation needed] Sunni Muslims however do not contest the parentage of her daughters.[citation needed] They affirm what the Qur'an states, "O Prophet! Tell your wives and your daughters and the women of the believers..."[4].
2 CONC [citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT The famous Sunni scholar Yusuf ibn abd al-Barr says: "His children born of Khadīja are four daughters; there is no difference of opinion about that".[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage rules
2 CONT
2 CONT Based on narrations found even in Sunnī[6] sources, Muhammad said that daughters of his household could only marry those who were from Banū Hāshim.[6].
2 CONT
2 CONT But(if it is assumed that Zainab, Ruqayyah and Umm Kulthum were daughter of Muhammed and Khadijah) this argument does not seem to be correct as:
2 CONT
2 CONT Zainab was married to Abu al-Aas ibn al-Rabee who belonged to Banu Abd Shams clan of the tribe Quraish. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruqayyah and after her death Umm Kulthum were married to Uthman bin Affan who belonged to Banu Umayya clan of the tribe Quraish[7]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Yasin T. Al-Jibouri, ''Khadijah, Daughter of Khuwaylid, Wife of Prophet Muhammad'' at al-Islam.org
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruqayyah_bint_Muhammad
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT uqayyah bint Muhammad (Arabic: رقية بنت محمد‎, Ruqayyah bint Muḥammad) (c.601 - 624) is regarded as the daughter of the Islamic prophet Muhammad and Khadija.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Mecca, apparently the fourth child of Muhammad and Khadija, so probably in 601 or 602.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married before August 610 to her cousin, Utbah ibn Abu Lahab, but the marriage was never consummated.[3] Ruqayyah became a Muslim when her mother did.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Muhammad began to preach openly in 613, the Quraysh reminded Muhammad that they had "relieved him of his care for his daughters" and decided to return them so that he would have to support them at his own expense. They told U
2 CONC tbah that they would give him "the daughter of Aban ibn Sa'id ibn Al-As or the daughter of Sa'id ibn Al-As" if he divorced Ruqayyah.[6] After Muhammad warned Abū Lahab that he would go to Hell, Abu Lahab said he would never speak 
2 CONC to his son again unless he divorced Ruqayyah, which Utbah accordingly did.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 615 Ruqayyah was married to a prominent Muslim, Uthman ibn Affan. She accompanied him on the first Migration to Abyssinia,[9][10][11] where she suffered a miscarriage. They returned to Abyssinia in 616, and there Ruqayyah gave 
2 CONC birth to a son, Abdullah, in 619. Abdullah died when he was six years old in Medina. She had no further children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uthman and Ruqayyah were among those who returned to Mecca in 619. Uthman emigrated to Medina in 622, and Ruqayyah followed him later.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruqayyah was said to be extremely beautiful. When Usama ibn Zayd was sent on an errand to their house, he found himself staring at her and at Uthman in turns. Muhammad asked Usama, "Have you ever seen a more handsome couple than t
2 CONC hose two?" and he agreed that he had not.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruqayyah fell ill in March 624. Uthman was excused from his military duties in order to nurse her. She died later in the month, on the day when Zayd ibn Harithah returned to Medina with news of their victory at the Battle of Badr.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Muhammad returned to Medina after the battle, the family went to grieve at her grave. The women wept noisily, and Umar hit them with his whip; but Muhammad said: "Let them weep, Umar; but beware of the braying of Satan."
2 CONT
2 CONT In several prominent Shia accounts Uthman is accused of beating Ruqayyah to death
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT •Khadijah bint Khuwaylid had two children named Hind, one with her first husband, Abu Hala, and one with her second husband, 'Atiq ibn 'Abid: 
2 CONT Hind bint Abi Hala: The son of Khadija and Abu Hala
2 CONT Hind bint 'Atiq: The daughter of Khadija and 'Atiq ibn 'Abid
2 CONT
2 CONT •google source: "Khadija bint Khuwaylid child hind"
2 CONT
2 CONT •To help resolve dispute.
2 CONT
2 CONT •how many marriages did Khadija bint Khuwaylid have?
2 CONT
2 CONT •Khadija bint Khuwaylid was married three times and had children from all of her marriages: 
2 CONT Atiq ibn 'A'idh ibn' Abdullah Al-Makhzumi: Khadija's first husband, to whom she had a daughter named Hindah 
2 CONT Malik ibn Nabash ibn Zargari ibn at-Tamimi: Khadija's second husband, to whom she had two sons named Hala and Hind 
2 CONT The Prophet Muhammad: Khadija's third and final husband, to whom she had at least six children 
2 CONT Khadija was a wealthy businesswoman and the first follower of Islam. She was often called the First Believer or the Mother of Believers.
