2 CONT Spouse(s) 	Shahrbanu bint Yazdegerd Umm Rubāb Umm Laylā Father 	Ali Mother 	Fatimah Children 	Ali ibn Husayn, Ali al-Akbar ibn Husayn, Ali al-Asghar ibn Husayn, Fatimah Kubra bint Hussain, Fatimah Sughra bint Hussain, Sukayna bint
2 CONC  Husayn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Panjetan.jpg Ali · Hasan · Husayn al-Sajjad · al-Baqir · al-Sadiq Musa (Twelver) · Ismail (Ismaili)
2 CONT
2 CONT Husayn ibn ‘Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib (Arabic: حسين بن علي بن أﺑﻲ طالب‎)‎ (3rd Sha'aban 4 AH - 10th Muharram 61 AH; 8 January 626 CE - 10 October 680 CE) was the son of ‘Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib (final Rashidun Caliph and first Shia Imam) and Fā
2 CONC timah Zahrā (daughter of the Islamic prophet Muhammad). Husayn is an important figure in Islam as he is a member of the Ahl al-Bayt (the household of Muhammad) and Ahl al-Kisa, as well as being a Shia Imam, and one of The Fourteen
2 CONC  Infallibles of Shia Twelvers. He is recognized as the 'Martyr Of Martyrs' by both Sunni and Shia[citation needed].
2 CONT
2 CONT Husayn ibn ‘Alī is exalted by all the Shia as a martyr who fought tyranny as he refused to pledge allegiance to Yazid I,[6] the Umayyad caliph. He rose up to create a regime that would reinstate a "true" Islamic polity as opposed 
2 CONC to what he considered the unjust rule of the Umayyads.[6] As a consequence, Husayn was killed and beheaded in the Battle of Karbala in 680 (61AH) by Shimr Ibn Thil-Jawshan.[7] The anniversary of his Shahid (martyrdom) is called As
2 CONC hura (tenth day of Muharram) and is a day of mourning for Shia Muslims.
2 CONT
2 CONT Revenge for Husayn's death was turned into a rallying cry that helped undermine the Umayyad Caliphate and gave impetus to the rise of a powerful Shia movement.[6] Contents [hide]
