1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Wilaadat (Birth): 20th Rajjab 56 AH (10th June 676 AD)
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death): 5th Rabi ul Aakhir 61 AH (4th January 681 AD)
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sukayna_bint_Husayn
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Wilaadat (Birth):
2 CONT * Wafaat (Death):
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sukayna_bint_Husayn
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Daughters_of_Husayn_ibn_Ali
2 CONT
2 CONT The story of Sakinah is one of the many emotional stories that Shī‘ī Muslims tell about Husayn and his martyrdom at the hands of Yazid's troops. The Battle of Karbala and the subsequent events at the court of Yazid are explained a
2 CONC nd mourned annually during the commemoration of the 10th of Muharram, also known as ‘Âshûrá’ (Arabic: عَـاشُـورَاء‎, tenth day). According to these religious narrations, Sakinah suffered from fatigue and thirst on the forced march
2 CONC  to Damascus, and later from cold and starvation in Yazid's dungeon.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Journey to Iraq and Shaam She accompanied her father when he traveled from Mecca to Kufah in Iraq. On the 2nd of Muharram, 61 AH (680 CE), Husain and 72 of his family members and companions were forced to camp in the plains of Kar
2 CONC bala by Yazid's army of 30,000 men. Yazid ibn Muawiyyah was the practical Caliph who desired religious authority by obtaining the allegiance of Husain, but the Imam would not give up his principles. On the 10th of Muharram, the Im
2 CONC am's household was attacked, a number of his companions were killed, and the survivors were made captives. The survivors included the Imam's sisters, wives, and daughters, including Sakinah, relatives of companions of the Imam, an
2 CONC d his son, Ali Zaynul-Abidin, who did not participate in the battle, due to an illness. Sakinah, as with others, had been grieved over the killings. They had also suffered from thirst.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The survivors were marched by Yazid's army from Karbala to Kufah, where Sakinah received water from a sympathetic woman, and then to Damascus in Shaam. There was a lack of pity from the captors' part during the journey. Even at th
2 CONC ese times of hardship and misery, Ruqayyah was sympathetic to others, such as her mother, whom she consoled her mother on the death of Ali al-Asghar.[8][9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and aftermath According to Shia Islamic narrations that are commemorated every year on the occasion of Ashura, after enduring the Battle of Karbala and the torturous journey to Damascus that followed it, Sukaynah died at the
2 CONC  age of four weeping over her father's head in Yazid palace hall where prisoner were initially stayed and, her body was originally buried at nearby site. Centuries later, an ʿĀlim (Arabic: عَالِم‎, Scholar) had a dream in which Sa
2 CONC kinah asked him to move her body from the grave to another site, due to water pouring into her grave. He and some people opened the grave, and saw that ground water was indeed entering the grave, besides that her body was still in
2 CONC tact. Sakinah's body was moved from its original burial place, the dungeon, and reburied where her Mosque is now located.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT The mosque was built around the mausoleum in 1985 and exhibits a modern version of Iranian architecture, with substantial amount of mirror and gold work. There is a small mosque area adjoining the shrine room, along with a small c
2 CONC ourtyard in front. This mosque is found a short distance from the Umayyad Mosque and the Al-Hamidiyah Souq in central Damascus.
