1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He is known in Arab-Islamic historical sources as Abdullah al-Jeeli, after al-Jeel, a historical village near al-Mada'in in Iraq, as confirmed by academic studies. He was known for his asceticism, piety, and devoti
2 CONC on. He had no descendants other than his son Abu Salih Musa, the father of Sheikh Abdul Qadir al-Jili, Muhammad, and Aisha. His wife was Umm Salama, the daughter of Muhammad ibn Talha ibn Abdullah ibn Abdul Rahman ibn Abi Bakr al-
2 CONC Siddiq (may God be pleased with them).
2 CONT
2 CONT Al-Safadi said: He was a renowned and generous leader, praised and long-lived. Ibn Shadaqam said in his book, "Tuhfa," regarding what was narrated from Sayyid Taj al-Din in "Hidayat al-Talib" with its chain of transmission to him:
2 CONC  He was a righteous, devout, pious, ascetic, knowledgeable, pious, virtuous, accomplished, eloquent, eloquent, literary, and a poet. Al-Bayhaqi said in "Al-Lubab": Abdullah was a virtuous and ascetic man who considered enjoining g
2 CONC ood and forbidding evil obligatory.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdullah used to recite poetry, and among his beautiful verses are these:
2 CONT
2 CONT "I am one who rides my horse and throws it, and myself, into the fray, for fear that this wretched world might sway me, as it swayed the wayward and the doomed.
2 CONT
2 CONT O Lord, if my death is near, do not let it be upon a high place adorned with green robes."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was among those distinguished for their precedence in opposing the oppressors, refusing to pledge allegiance to them, and abandoning their followers. He was one of the Imams of the Ahlul-Bayt, and one of those qualified for the
2 CONC  Imamate.
