1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sayyid Jalaluddin Surkh-Posh Naqvi Al Bukhari (Persian: سید جلال الدین سرخ پوش بخاری, c. 595-690 AH, 1190 – 1295 CE was a saint from the Indian subcontinent. He belonged to the Jalali Sufi order and was descended f
2 CONC rom the 10th Shia Imam, Ali al-Hadi.
2 CONT Bukhari, a family name, is derived from the location of his birth from the city of Bukhara in the ancient administrative region of Bukhara Province Uzbekistan.[2] Sayyed Jalaluddin Bukhari, who was born in Bukhara but later settle
2 CONC d in Bukkur located in sindh alongside his in laws descendant from Muhammad Al-Makki. However, after a dispute with his father in laws brothers, he further migrated with his father in law (Sayyid Badruddin) to Uch in undivided Ind
2 CONC ia.[3] Bukhari was nicknamed Surkh-Posh ("clad in red") because he often wore a red cape.[1]
2 CONT Death
2 CONT edit
2 CONT In 1244 CE (about 640AH), Bukhari moved to Uch, South Punjab (also known as Uch Sharif after the saint settled there) with his son, Baha-ul-Halim, where he founded a religious school. He died in 1290 CE and was buried in a small t
2 CONC own near Uch.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Mosque (left) and entrance to the tomb/shrine of Jalaluddin Bukhari (right) in Uch
2 CONT The tomb is a short way from the cemetery of Uch. It stands on a promontory overlooking the plains and the desert beyond. To one side of the tomb is a mosque decorated with blue tilework. In front of the tomb is a pool. A carved w
2 CONC ooden door leads into the room containing Bukhari's coffin. UNESCO describes the site:
2 CONT
2 CONT The brick-built tomb measures 18 meters by 24 meters and its carved wooden pillars support a flat roof and it is decorated with glazed tiles in floral and geometric designs. The ceiling is painted with floral designs in lacquer an
2 CONC d its floor is covered with the graves of the saint and his relatives an interior partition provides 'purdah' for those of his womenfolk. Its mosque consists of a hall, measuring 20 meters by 11 meters, with 18 wooden pillars supp
2 CONC orting a flat roof. It was built of cut and dressed bricks and further decorated, internally and externally, with enamelled tiles in floral and geometric designs.[6]
