1 OCCU Leader of Sufi Order 1293-1334
1 TITL Sultan
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safi-ad-din_Ardabili
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi-ad-din Is'haq Ardabili (of Ardabil) (1252–1334) (Persian: شیخ صفی‌الدین اسحاق اردبیلی‎‎ Shaikh Ṣāfī ad-Dīn Isḥāq Ardabīlī), was the Kurdish[2][3][4] and Sunni[1] Muslim eponym of the Safavid dynasty, founder of the Saf
2 CONC aviyya order, and the spiritual heir and son in law of the great Sufi Murshid (Grand Master) Sheikh Zahed Gilani, of Lahijan in Gilan province in northern Iran. Most of what we know about him comes from the Safvat as-safa, a hagio
2 CONC graphy written by one of his followers. Safi-ad-din was of Kurdish origins. According to Minorsky, Sheykh Safi al-Din's ancestor Firuz-shah was a rich man, lived in Gilan and then Kurdish kings gave him Ardabil and its dependencie
2 CONC s. Minorsky refers to Sheykh Safi al-Din's claims tracing back his origins to Ali ibn Abu Talib, but expresses uncertainty about this.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT The male lineage of the Safavid family given by the oldest manuscript of the Safwat as-Safa is:"(Shaykh) Safi al-Din Abul-Fatah Ishaaq the son of Al-Shaykh Amin al-din Jebrail the son of al-Saaleh Qutb al-Din Abu Bakr the son of S
2 CONC alaah al-Din Rashid the son of Muhammad al-Hafiz al-Kalaam Allah the son of Javaad the son of Pirooz al-Kurdi al-Sanjani (Piruz Shah Zarin Kolah the Kurd of Sanjan)"[6] similar to the ancestry of Sheykh Safi al-Din's father in law
2 CONC , Sheikh Zahed Gilani, who also hailed from Sanjan, in Greater Khorassan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi al-Din inherited Sheikh Zahed Gilani's Sufi order, the "Zahediyeh", which he later transformed into his own, the "Safaviyya". Sheikh Zahed Gilani also gave his daughter Bibi Fatemeh in wedlock to his favorite disciple.
2 CONC  Sheikh Safi al-Din, in turn, gave a daughter from a previous marriage in wedlock to Shaikh Zahed Gilani's second-born son. Over the following 170 years, the Safaviyya Order gained political and military power, finally culminating
2 CONC  in the foundation of the Safavid dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT He wed Sheikh Zahed's daughter Bibi Fatima and, overgoing the interest of Zahed's firstborn son, Gamal Al-Din Ali, was entrusted with the Grand Master's Zahediyeh Sufi Order, which he transformed into his own, the Safaviyya (Sufi 
2 CONC order) Order
2 CONT
2 CONT Poetry Sheikh Safi al-Din has composed poems in the Iranian dialect of old Tati.[7][8] He was a seventh-generation descendant of Firuz-Shah Zarrin-Kolah, a local Iranian dignitary.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Only a very few verses of Sheikh Safi al-Din's poetry, called Dobaytis (double verses), have survived. Written in old Tati and Persian, they have linguistic importance today.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Safi-ad-din_Ardabili
2 CONT ====
2 CONT (Safi al-Din Abul-Fatah Ishaaq)
2 CONT
2 CONT He is Persian and Kurdish.
2 CONT Sheikh Safi and his son Sadreddin were Shafii Sunnis but the Safavids were Shi'ites from the times of Khajeh Ali Siahpoush https://www.geni.com/people/Sheikh-Ali-Safavi-Sultan/6000000008540153460. (according to H.R. Roemer, The Sa
2 CONC favid Period, in Cambridge History of Iran, Vol. VI, Cambridge University )
2 CONT =====
2 CONT Link to the seventh imaam Shi'ite: 
2 CONT His father in law, Sheikh Zahed Gilani, a13th century spiritual leader (1216-1301) recognized Sheikh Safi eddin Ardebili as his successor and gave him the hand of his daughter in marriage. The name Saffaviya is derived from the Sh
2 CONC eikh's name Safi. Safi claimed he was a descendent of the Prophet Mohammad through the seventh imam of the Shiites, Musa el Qazem https://www.geni.com/people/Imaam-Musa-al-Kaazim-bin-Imaam-Ja-afar-us-Saadiq/4752719506900128751 Thi
2 CONC s claim was passed onto Safi's descendants, including the founder of the Safavid Dynasty, Shah Ismail.
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT Sheikh Safi-ad-din Is'haq Ardabili (of Ardabil) (1252–1334) (Persian: شیخ صفی‌الدین اردبیلی‎‎), was the Kurdish and Sunni Muslim eponym of the Safavid dynasty, founder of the Safaviyya order, and the spiritual heir and son in law 
2 CONC of the great Sufi Murshid (Grand Master) Sheikh Zahed Gilani, of Lahijan in Gilan province in northern Iran. Most of what we know about him comes from the Safvat as-safa, a hagiography written by one of his followers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi al-Din's has composed poems in the Iranian dialect of old Tati.  He was a seventh-generation descendant of Firuz-Shah Zarrin-Kolah, a local Iranian dignitary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi al-Din inherited Sheikh Zahed Gilani's Sufi order, the "Zahediyeh", which he later transformed into his own, the "Safaviyya". Sheikh Zahed Gilani also gave his daughter Bibi Fatemeh in wedlock to his favorite disciple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi al-Din, in turn, gave a daughter from a previous marriage in wedlock to Shaikh Zahed Gilani's second-born son. Over the following 170 years, the Safaviyya Order gained political and military power, finally culminating 
2 CONC in the foundation of the Safavid dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sheikh Safi al-Din went over the interest of Zahed's firstborn son, Gamal Al-Din Ali, was entrusted with the Grand Master's Zahediyeh Sufi Order, which he transformed into his own, the Safaviyya (Sufi order) Order. Zahed Gilani's 
2 CONC second-born son, Sadr al-Dīn, wed Safi Al-Din's daughter from a previous marriage. Some 170 years after Safi Al-Din's death, Safaviyya had gained sufficient political and military power to claim the Throne of (Northern) Iran for t
2 CONC he Safavid Heir, Shah Ismail I Safavi. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The two families were to be intertwined for many centuries to come, by blood as well as mutual spiritual causes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Only a very few verses of Sheikh Safi al-Din's poetry, called Dobaytis (double verses), have survived. Written in old Tati and Persian, they have linguistic importance today.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Minorsky, Sheykh Safi al-Din's ancestor Firuz-shah was a rich man, lived in Gilan and then Kurdish kings gave him Ardabil and its dependencies. Minorsky refers to Sheykh Safi al-Din's claims tracing back his origins t
2 CONC o Ali ibn Abu Talib, but expresses uncertainty about this.
2 CONT
2 CONT The male lineage of the Safavid family given by the oldest manuscript of the Safwat as-Safa is:"(Shaykh) Safi al-Din Abul-Fatah Ishaaq the son of Al-Shaykh Amin al-din Jebrail the son of al-Saaleh Qutb al-Din Abu Bakr the son of S
2 CONC alaah al-Din Rashid the son of Muhammad al-Hafiz al-Kalaam Allah the son of Javaad the son of Pirooz al-Kurdi al-Sanjani (Piruz Shah Zarin Kolah the Kurd of Sanjan)" similar to the ancestry of Sheykh Safi al-Din's father in law, S
2 CONC heikh Zahed Gilani, who also hailed from Sanjan, in Greater Khorassan.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Other transliterations for Safi al-Din: Safi al-Din, Safi ad-Dîn, Safi Eddin, Safi od-Din, Safi El-Din, Safieddin, Safioddin)
2 CONT
2 CONT Although there are no confirmed records, the language called in Iranian linguistics as Azari can be the antecedent of both Talyshi and Tati. Miller’s (1953) hypothesis that the Âzari of Ardabil, as appears in the quatrains of Shai
2 CONC kh Safi, was a form of Talyshi was confirmed by Henning (1954). In western literature the people and the language are sometimes referred to as Talishi, Taleshi or Tolashi. Generally speaking, written documents about Taleshi are ra
2 CONC re.
